15
00:07:38.000 --> 00:08:07.000
So we should do a series at some point on the troubles specifically. At this point, hundreds of people, 250, 300, are dying every year. So a real running sore. And all of that came to ahead, as we described in our previous series, in the tumultuous year of 1974. The oil shock of late 1973 basically blew up the British economy. Well, thank God, nothing like that's going to happen now. It sent inflation through the roof, and this will be a constant theme of this series.
所以通货膨胀在1975年夏天达到了创纪录的26%。在70年代初担任首相的现代化保守党议员泰德·希思，他失去了对经济的控制。他试图实施一种工资政策，但这引发了煤矿工人的罢工。他在2月举行了选举，试图从人民那里获得新的授权。结果陷入了僵局，形成了一个悬浮议会。他被那个非常“旧货”的人物，前首相、工党的哈罗德·威尔逊所取代。威尔逊收买了工会。

16
00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:40.000
So inflation reached a record 26% in the summer of 1975. The modernising Tory MP, Ted Heath, who was Prime Minister at the beginning of the 70s, he lost control of the economy. He tried to impose a kind of pay policy, but that provoked a strike by the coal mine. He called an election in February to try to get a new mandate from the people. And that ended in a deadlock in a hung parliament, so stalemate. And he was replaced by the very shop-soiled figure, the former Prime Minister, Labour's Harold Wilson. And Wilson paid off the unions.
他带来了工业和平，但通货膨胀变得更糟了。对威尔逊来说，好消息是他10月又举行了一次选举，并以仅仅三个席位的微弱优势获胜。这一切的结果是，特别是在右翼，实际上在国外，到1975年初，人们有一种深刻的感觉，即事情已经变得非常、非常糟糕。英国是“欧洲病夫”，正走向某种末日般的清算。因为你在我们关于1974年的上一系列中提到，有各种各样的人在策划政变，不是吗？是的，将军，他叫什么名字？

17
00:08:40.000 --> 00:09:14.000
He brought industrial peace, but inflation got worse. The upside for Wilson, he calls another election in October, and he eked out a very narrow majority of just three seats. And the result of all this is that, especially on the right, and indeed abroad, there is a profound sense by the beginning of 1975 that something has gone very, very badly wrong. The Britain is the sick man of Europe and it's heading for some sort of apocalyptic reckoning. Because you mentioned in our previous series on 1974 that there are all these kind of people who were plotting coups, weren't there? Yes, General, what's his name?
沃尔特·沃克爵士还是什么名字。是的，他们所有人。还有大卫·斯特林，那个创立了SAS的人，所有这些自愿担任民族团结政府领导人的人。我给你举一个国际认知的例子。1975年初，美国资深记者埃里克·塞瓦雷德在美国哥伦比亚广播公司晚间新闻中发表了一篇报道。塞瓦雷德对观众说，英国的问题不仅仅是她的军事力量正在减弱，经济力量正在衰退。

18
00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:45.000
Sir Walter Walker or whatever's name is. Yeah, all of them. There's David Sterling, the guy who founded the SAS, all these people who are sort of offering themselves. as leaders of a national unity government. I'll give you one example of the international perception. At the beginning of 1975, there was a report on the CBS Evening News in the United States by the veteran American correspondent Eric Severide. And Severide said to the viewers, Britain's problem is not just that her military strength is ebbing and her economic strength weakening.
英国正处于阿连德智利政府的阶段，当时少数派试图将深刻的社会变革强加给多数派。它正在缓慢地漂向一种不可治理的状态，并梦游般地走向一场社会革命。这足以成为一个新闻，哈罗德·威尔逊发表了一份声明，称这完全是不真实的等等。泰晤士报为此发表了一篇长篇社论。它说，你知道，实际上我们没有阿连德的智利那么糟糕，但我们确实很糟糕。我们借了太多的钱。我们在世界舞台上是笑柄。

19
00:09:45.000 --> 00:10:17.000
Britain is at the stage of Allende's Chilean government when a minority tried to force a profound transformation of society upon the majority. It's drifting slowly toward a condition of ungovernability and sleepwalking into a social revolution. And this was enough of a story that Harold Wilson issued a statement saying this was completely untrue and whatnot. And the Times published a long editorial about it. And it said, you know, actually we're not as bad as Ianda's chilly, but we are pretty bad. We're borrowing far too much money. We're porpoise on the world stage.
泰晤士报最后说，这些事实全世界都知道，英国的声望自查理二世作为路易十四的养老金领取者以来，从未如此低落。我的意思是，多米尼克，这又有一种当前情况的回响，不是吗？因为目前英国报纸上有这么多关于我们的军事地位处于17世纪状态的文章。是的，完全是。我的意思是，所有这些美国对英国的辱骂是非常普遍的。这是一种常青的主题。有一种特定的美国记者，最喜欢做的事就是踢前主人一脚。

20
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:49.000
And the Times ended up by saying these facts are well known to the world where British prestige has not stood so low since child. Charles the second was the pensioner of Louis the 14th. I mean, again, Dominic, there is a kind of echo of present circumstance, isn't there? Because there are so many articles in the British newspapers at the moment about how our military standing is kind of in a 17th century state. Yeah, completely. I mean, this is all the stuff, this American abuse of Britain is very common. It's kind of perennial theme. There's a particular kind of American correspondent that loves nothing better than putting the boot into the former master.
